! Storage protection: the permanently protected area, gated on the
! interrupt-system enable bit.
!
! THE RULES, all from the Reference Manual.
!
! p.31: "The monitoring of references to locations 0-31 is independent
! of the contents of the boundary registers.  Locations 1, 2, and 3 are
! referred to as the permanently protected area of storage.  References
! to these locations always give an address monitoring signal,
! regardless of the contents of the boundary registers or the setting
! of the boundary control bit.  References to locations 0 and 4 through
! 31, inclusive, never cause an address monitoring signal."
!
! p.32, on a STORE specifically: "Since this indicator [DS] is
! permanently masked on, storing in core storage is always suppressed,
! and the operation is always terminated."  DS is bit 19 and mask bits
! 0-19 are permanently one, so there is no mask-dependent branch here,
! unlike DF and IF.
!
! p.45: the indicator means a store was attempted "WHILE THE
! INTERRUPTION SYSTEM WAS ENABLED".  So protection is gated on the
! enable bit -- which is why that bit exists in this model at all, and
! why it is the smallest piece of the interrupt system that a program
! can observe.
!
! THE DECK AGREES, AND SAID SO FIRST.  SSIP's SIP16
! (ssip_sip17_reset.strap) rewrites words 1, 2 and 3 -- the interval
! timer, the interruption address register and the boundary registers
! -- and a BD guards the sequence, commented "DISABLE BEFORE RESET OF
! PROTECTED AREA".  A program that needs to write protected storage
! turns the system off first.  This program is that behaviour in two
! directions.
!
! AND A DECK TESTS EXACTLY THIS RULE.  jb_ax5b routine 1260's block
! I6086 (pp.43-44, transcribed as ax5b_16086_ds.strap and checked
! bit-exactly) stores into WORD 1 and reports, if DS stays off:
!
!     "SV TO 1.0 FAILS TO SET DS WHEN ENABLED."
!
! Word 1 rather than the word 3 this program picked -- between them
! the pair p.31 names -- the DS indicator, and the "when enabled"
! gate, all three in the deck's own words.  It also reads DS only
! AFTER "I6086A BD,$+.32" disables the system, which is the same
! reason this program observes the violation through the interruption
! routine instead of polling.  That transcription is ENCODING-only
! (the block needs I6085B's NOP table and the SEN/SERS harness to
! run), so this program is still the behavioural test and still
! hand-written -- but the rule it asserts is no longer only my
! reading of the manual.
!
! WHY WORD 3 AND BRANCH ON BIT.  Word 3 is in the permanently protected
! set, so no boundary registers need setting -- and none are
! implemented.  BB both writes and reads a single bit, so one
! instruction type shows the store's effect and the absence of it.
!
! KNOWN DEVIATION, STATED: p.32 says the whole operation is
! "terminated", and this model suppresses only the STORE -- a BB whose
! write is suppressed still takes its branch.  Nothing transcribed
! distinguishes the two, and the branch is not what DS reports.
!
! WEAKER STANDARD: hand-written from p.31/p.32/p.45.  The deck block it
! mirrors is transcribed separately and is encoding-only.

! A PROGRAM CANNOT POLL DS.  This program used to read DS directly,
! with "BZB,$DS,$", and that was only ever possible because the
! interruption mechanism did not exist yet.  DS is indicator 19 and
! mask bits 0-19 are permanently 1 (p.48), so the moment an ENABLED
! machine raises DS it takes an interruption -- and the mechanism
! "turns off" that indicator before forming the address (p.48).  By the
! time the next instruction runs, DS is gone.  That is also why SSIP
! polls indicators with the system DISABLED.
!
! So the violation is observed the way the machine actually reports it:
! through the interruption routine at table entry 19.  This program is
! strictly stronger for it -- it now witnesses the suppression AND the
! interruption that p.32 and p.48 together require, where before it saw
! only a bit.
!
! THE MARKER LIVES IN WORD 4, and that is not arbitrary.  p.31:
! locations "0 and 4 through 31, inclusive, never cause an address
! monitoring signal".  Word 3's bit 0 is set below, which makes the
! upper boundary 131072 and puts every ordinary address inside the
! monitored area -- so a marker anywhere in core would itself be
! suppressed and raise DS again, forever.  Word 4 cannot.

        SLC, (8)4100.0
!
! ---- disabled: the store into protected storage SUCCEEDS -----------
! Disabled is also the reset state (p.53), so the BD is belt and
! braces -- and it is what SIP16 does before its own protected writes.
START   BD, $+.32                    ! disable the interrupt system
        LVI, $X13, TBL               ! the interruption table origin
        SV,  $X13, $IA               ! word 2 is protected: disabled first,
                                     !   exactly as SIP16 does it
        BB1, 3.0, $+1.0              ! set bit 0 of word 3
        BZB, 3.0, $                  ! hang up if bit 0 is off: the store
                                     !   must have landed
        BB, 6.0, $                   ! hang up if DS's routine has run:
                                     !   nothing was protected while disabled
!
! ---- enabled: the same store is SUPPRESSED and DS interrupts -------
        BE, $+.32                    ! enable the interrupt system
        BBZ, 3.0, $+1.0              ! try to CLEAR bit 0 of word 3
        BZB, 3.0, $                  ! hang up if bit 0 is off -- it must
                                     !   still be set, the store suppressed
        BZB, 6.0, $                  ! hang up if DS's routine did NOT run
        B, PASS
PASS    BEW, $
!
! ---- the interruption table ----------------------------------------
        SLC, (8)4400.0
TBL     DD, 0                        ! entry N is at TBL+N words
        SLC, (8)4423.0
DSRTN   BB1, 6.0, $+1.0              ! entry 19 = DS: record the violation
                                     !   in a word that can never signal
        END, START
